(i)=1$ (resp. $p(i)=n$).\\n\\nEvery unit square of the board belongs either to some $L_i$, to some $R_i$, or is the uncovered square $(i,p(i))$.  Moreover, a rectangular tile cannot contain any uncovered square, so each tile is either entirely contained in $\\\\bigcup_i L_i$ (a *left tile*) or entirely in $\\\\bigcup_i R_i$ (a *right tile*).  A tile can never contain squares from both $L_i$ and $R_i$ of the same row, because the uncovered square separates those two regions.\\n\\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the number of left tiles\\n\\nFor each row $i$ with $p(i)>1$ the cell\\n\\\\[\\nc_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\\\in L_i\\n\\\\]\\nis the **rightmost** cell of $L_i$; we call $c_i$ a *left‑corner*.  The $n-1$ rows with $p(i)>1$ give $n-1$ distinct left‑corners, because the column numbers $p(i)-1$ run through $\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots ,n-1\\\\}$ exactly once.\\n\\n\\\\**Lemma 1.**\\\\* A left tile can contain at most one left‑corner.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Let $T$ be a left tile.  Since $T$ is a rectangle, its set of columns is an interval $[1,k]$ for some $k\\\\ge 1$ (the left side of a left tile must start at column $1$, otherwise it would intersect the uncovered square of a row whose missing column lies to the left of the tile).  Suppose $T$ contained two distinct left‑corners $c_i=(i,p(i)-1)$ and $c_{i'}=(i',p(i')-1)$ with $i\\\\neq i'$.  Because $c_i\\\\in T$, we have $k\\\\ge p(i)-1$; because $T$ must avoid the uncovered square $(i,p(i))$, we also need $k\\\\le p(i)-1$.  Hence $k=p(i)-1$.  The same reasoning applied to $c_{i'}$ yields $k=p(i')-1$.  Consequently $p(i)=p(i')$, contradicting the fact that $p$ is a permutation.  ∎\\n\\nSince the $n-1$ left‑corners are pairwise distinct, Lemma 1 implies that at least $n-1$ left tiles are necessary.\\n\\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the number of right tiles\\n\\nDefine for each row $i$ with $p(i)<n$ the **leftmost** cell of $R_i$,\\n\\\\[\\ns_i=(i,p(i)+1)\\\\in R_i,\\n\\\\]\\nand call it a *right‑corner*.  The column numbers $p(i)+1$ run through $\\\\{2,3,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$, so there are again $n-1$ distinct right‑corners.\\n\\n\\\\**Lemma 2.**\\\\* A right tile can contain at most one right‑corner.\\n\\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric to Lemma 1.  A right tile must have its column interval of the form $[\\\\ell,n]$ with $\\\\ell\\\\ge 1$.  If it contained two right‑corners $(i,p(i)+1)$ and $(i',p(i')+1)$, the same comparison of the leftmost column $\\\\ell$ would force $p(i)=p(i')$, a contradiction.  ∎\\n\\nHence at least $n-1$ right tiles are required.\\n\\n#### 4.  Overall lower bound\\n\\nAny admissible tiling consists of left tiles together with right tiles, so the total number $T$ of tiles satisfies\\n\\\\[\\nT\\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ this gives $T\\\\ge 4048$.\\n\\n#### 5.  A tiling that meets the bound\\n\\nWe now exhibit a tiling using exactly $2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\nFor each row $i$:\\n* If $p(i)>1$, place a **left tile** $L_i$ that covers the whole set $L_i$, i.e. the rectangle\\n  \\\\[L_i=\\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots ,p(i)-1\\\\}.\\n  This tile has height $1$ and width $p(i)-1$.\\n* If $p(i)<n$, place a **right tile** $R_i$ that covers $R_i$, i.e. the rectangle\\n  \\\\[R_i=\\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{p(i)+1,p(i)+2,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}.\\n  This tile also has height $1$.\\n\\nEach $L_i$ (resp. $R_i$) lies completely to the left (resp. to the right) of the uncovered square $(i,p(i))$, so it never contains an uncovered square.  Different rows are disjoint, therefore all these tiles are pairwise disjoint.  The uncovered squares themselves are left uncovered, as required.\\n\\nHow many tiles are used?  Exactly one left tile for every row with $p(i)>1$, i.e. for $n-1$ rows, and one right tile for every row with $p(i)<n$, again $n-1$ rows.  Consequently the total number of tiles equals\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ this construction uses $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$ tiles.\\n\\n#### 6.  Optimality\\n\\nThe lower bound of Section 4 shows that no tiling can use fewe