lower bound and the construction coincide, therefore $2n-2$ is the minimum possible number of tiles.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  Index the rows and columns of the $n\\\\times n$ board by $1,2,\\\\dots ,n$.\\n\\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares\\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.  Hence there exists a permutation $p$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$ such that the unique uncovered square in row $i$ is the cell\\n\\\\[ u_i=(i, p(i)). \\\\]\\nFor a fixed row $i$ define the *left part*\\n\\\\[ L_i=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le j<p(i)\\\\}, \\\\]\\nand the *right part*\\n\\\\[ R_i=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid p(i)<j\\\\le n\\\\}. \\\\]\\nIf $p(i)=1$ then $L_i$ is empty; if $p(i)=n$ then $R_i$ is empty.\\nEvery unit square of the board belongs either to some $L_i$, to some $R_i$, or is an uncovered square $u_i$.\\nA rectangular tile cannot contain any $u_i$, therefore each tile is either completely contained in $\\\\bigcup_i L_i$ (we call it a *left tile*) or completely contained in $\\\\bigcup_i R_i$ (a *right tile*).  A tile can never contain squares from both $L_i$ and $R_i$ of the same row, because the uncovered square separates those two regions.\\n\\n#### 2.  Left\u2011corners and a lower bound for left tiles\\nFor each row with $p(i)>1$ define the **left\u2011corner**\\n\\\\[ c_i=(i, p(i)-1)\\\\in L_i. \\\\]\\nThe set $\\\\{c_i\\\\mid p(i)>1\\\\}$ has exactly $n-1$ elements, because the column numbers $p(i)-1$ run through $\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots ,n-1\\\\}$ exactly once.\\n\\n**Lemma\u202f1.** A left tile can contain at most one left\u2011corner.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Let $T$ be a left tile.  Its set of columns is an interval $[a,b]$ with $a\\\\le b$.  Since $T$ is left of the uncovered square in every row it covers, we have $b<p(i)$ for each such row $i$.  If $T$ contains a left\u2011corner $c_i=(i,p(i)-1)$, then $p(i)-1\\\\le b$ (because the corner lies in $T$) and $b<p(i)$.  Hence $b=p(i)-1$.  If $T$ also contained a different left\u2011corner $c_j$, the same reasoning would give $b=p(j)-1$, whence $p(i)=p(j)$, contradicting that $p$ is a permutation.  Therefore $T$ cannot contain two distinct left\u2011corners.  \u220e\\n\\nSince each left\u2011corner must be covered by some left tile (a right tile cannot contain a cell left of its row\u2019s uncovered square), Lemma\u202f1 implies that at least $n-1$ left tiles are necessary.\\n\\n#### 3.  Right\u2011corners and a lower bound for right tiles\\nFor each row with $p(i)<n$ define the **right\u2011corner**\\n\\\\[ d_i=(i, p(i)+1)\\\\in R_i. \\\\]\\nAgain there are exactly $n-1$ right\u2011corners, because the column numbers $p(i)+1$ run through $\\\\{2,3,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$.\\n\\n**Lemma\u202f2.** A right tile can contain at most one right\u2011corner.\\n\\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric to Lemma\u202f1.  If a right tile $T$ has column interval $[a,b]$, then for every row it covers we have $a>p(i)$.  Containing a right\u2011corner $d_i=(i,p(i)+1)$ forces $a=p(i)+1$, and two distinct right\u2011corners would force $p(i)=p(j)$, a contradiction.  \u220e\\n\\nConsequently at least $n-1$ right tiles are required.\\n\\n#### 4.  Overall lower bound\\nEvery admissible tiling consists of left tiles together with right tiles, so the total number $N$ of tiles satisfies\\n\\\\[ N\\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ this yields the inequality $N\\\\ge 4048$.\\n\\n#### 5.  A tiling attaining the bound\\nWe now exhibit a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.\\nFor each row $i$:\\n* If $p(i)>1$, place a **left tile** $L_i$ covering the whole set $L_i$, i.e. the rectangle\\n  \\\\[ L_i=\\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots ,p(i)-1\\\\}. \\\\]\\n  This tile has height $1$ and width $p(i)-1$.\\n* If $p(i)<n$, place a **right tile** $R_i$ covering $R_i$, i.e. the rectangle\\n  \\\\[ R_i=\\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{p(i)+1,p(i)+2,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}. \\\\]\\n  This tile also has height $1$.\\nEach $L_i$ (resp. $R_i$) lies completely to the left (resp. to the right) of the uncovered square $u_i$, so it never contains a hole.  Different rows are disjoint, therefore all the tiles $L_i$ and $R_i$ are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square of $\\\\bigcup_i L_i\\\\cup\\\\bigcup_i R_i$, i.e. every square that is not an uncovered one.\\nHow many tiles are used?  Exactly one left tile for each row with $p(i)>1$, i.e. for $n-1$ rows, and one right tile for each row with $p(i)<n$, again $n-1$ rows.  Hence the total number of tiles equals\\n\\\\[ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ this construction uses $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$ tiles.\\n\\n#### 6.  Optimality\\nThe lower bound of Section\u202f4 shows that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, while the construction of Section\u202f5 achieves exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Therefore $2n-2$ is the minimum possible number of tiles.\\n\\nFor the given board ($n=2025$) the minimum number of tiles is\\n\\\\[ 2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048. \\\\]\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Answer:** The minimum number of tiles required is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
