 and 1x(n-p(i)) right tile. These are disjoint because they are in different rows. Within a row, left tile and right tile are separated by the uncovered square. So they are disjoint. So the construction is valid.\n\nNow we need to check the lower bound: The argument that each left tile can contain at most one left corner seems correct. However, we need to double-check the definition of left tile: \"A rectangular tile cannot contain any u_i, therefore each tile is either completely contained in union of L_i (left tile) or completely contained in union of R_i (right tile).\" Is this always true? Let's think: The uncovered squares are at positions (i, p(i)). The tile cannot contain any uncovered square. Could a tile contain squares from both left and right of the same row? The solution claims \"A tile can never contain squares from both L_i and R_i of the same row, because the uncovered square separates those two regions.\" Indeed, if a tile includes squares from both sides of the uncovered square in the same row, then it would have to include the uncovered square itself because the tile is a rectangle. Since the tile's rows are contiguous intervals, and columns are contiguous intervals, if the tile includes a cell left of column p(i) and a cell right of column p(i) in the same row i, then the rectangle spanning those columns includes column p(i) as well, thus includes the uncovered square (i, p(i)), which is not allowed. So indeed any tile cannot cross the uncovered square in a row. However, could a tile be partially in left side of some rows and partially in right side of other rows? That is, a tile could be tall, covering multiple rows, and for some rows its column interval lies left of the uncovered square, for other rows it lies right. But the tile's column interval is the same across all rows (since it's a rectangle). So if the column interval includes some columns left of p(i) for some row i, and also includes some columns right of p(j) for some other row j, then the column interval must include columns that are both left of p(i) and right of p(j). If p(i) and p(j) are different, the column interval might cross the uncovered squares of some rows. Let's examine: Suppose we have a tile with column interval [a,b]. For each row i that the tile covers, we must have either b < p(i) (tile lies entirely left of uncovered square in that row) or a > p(i) (tile lies entirely right). So for each row i covered, either p(i) > b or p(i) < a. So the set of rows covered can be partitioned into those where p(i) > b (tile left) and those where p(i) < a (tile right). If both types exist, then there exist rows i and j such that p(i) > b and p(j) < a. Since a <= b, we have p(i) > b >= a > p(j). So p(i) > p(j). That's fine. But does that cause a problem? The tile's column interval [a,b] is fixed. For row i where p(i) > b, the tile is left of the uncovered square. For row j where p(j) < a, the tile is right of the uncovered square. So the tile can be left of uncovered squares for some rows and right for others. However, the solution's claim \"each tile is either completely contained in union of L_i (left tile) or completely contained in union of R_i (right tile)\" asserts that a tile cannot have both left and right rows. Is that true? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have a permutation p. The uncovered squares are at (i, p(i)). For a tile T with column interval [a,b] and row interval [r1, r2] (i.e., rows i from r1 to r2 inclusive). For each row i in [r1,r2], we need either b < p(i) (tile left) or a > p(i) (tile right). If there exist rows i and j such that b < p(i) and a > p(j), then we have b < p(i) and a > p(j). Since a <= b, we get a <= b < p(i) and a > p(j). So p(j) < a <= b < p(i). So p(j) < p(i). That's possible. So the tile could be left of uncovered squares for rows with larger p(i) and right of uncovered squares for rows with smaller p(j). So the tile could be left for some rows and right for others. Does the solution's argument forbid this? Let's check the reasoning: \"A rectangular tile cannot contain any u_i, therefore each tile is either completely contained in ∪ L_i (we call it a left tile) or completely contained in ∪ R_i (a right tile). A tile can never contain squares from both L_i and R_i of the same row, because the uncovered square separates those two regions.\" The phrase \"cannot contain squares from both L_i and R_i of the same row\" is correct. But they then claim \"therefore each tile is either completely contained in ∪ L_i or completely contained in ∪ R_i\". This is a stronger claim: they assert that a tile cannot have squares from L_i for some rows and from R_j for other rows. But is that true? Let's think: Suppose we have a tile that covers rows i and j. In row i, the tile's columns are left of p(i). In row j, the tile's columns are right of p(j). Then the tile's column interval must be both left of p(i) and right of p(j). Since p(i) and p(j) are different, it's possible if p(j) < a <= b < p(i). So the tile's column interval lies between p(j) and p(i). But then for row i, the tile's column interval is left of p(i) (since b < p(i)). For row j, the tile's column interval is right of p(j) (since a > p(j)). So the tile is left for row i and right for row j. This seems allowed by the constraints: The tile does not contain any uncovered squares because for each row it stays on one side. The tile's column interval does not intersect any uncovered squares because for row i, uncovered square is at column p(i) > b; for row j, uncovered square is at column p(j) < a. So the tile does not contain any uncovered squares. So the tile is allowed. However, does it intersect any uncovered squares in other rows? For any row k in the tile's row interval, we need either b < p(k) or a > p(k). If p(k) lies between a and b (i.e., a <= p(k) <= b), then the tile would contain (k, p(k)), which is not allowed. So the tile's column interval must avoid all p(k) for rows k in its row interval. So the tile's column interval must be a gap in the set of p-values for those rows. Since p is a permutation, the set of p-values for rows in the tile's row interval is a set of distinct numbers. If there is a gap between the min and max of those p-values, the tile could occupy that gap. For example, if the rows covered have p-values {2,5}, then the tile could have column interval [3,4] (a=3, b=4). That interval does not contain any p-values (2 or 5). So the tile would be left of p=5 and right of p=2. So indeed a tile could be left for some rows and right for others. So the solution's claim that each tile is either left tile or right tile exclusively is false in general.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that each left tile can contain at most one left corner might still hold, but the classification of tiles into left and right might be incomplete. The lower bound argument uses left corners: left corners are cells (i, p(i)-1) for rows where p(i) > 1. They claim each left tile can contain at most one left corner. But if a tile can be a \"mixed\" tile (left for some rows, right for others), could it contain multiple left corners? Possibly. Let's analyze: Suppose a tile has column interval [a,b] and row interval [r1,r2]. For each row i in this interval, either b < p(i) (tile left) or a > p(i) (tile right). For a left corner c_i = (i, p(i)-1) to be in the tile, we need p(i)-1 to be in [a,b] and also the tile must be left for that row (i.e., b < p(i)). Since p(i)-1 <= b < p(i), we have b = p(i)-1. So for a left corner to be in the tile, the tile's rightmost column b must equal p(i)-1. So the tile's column interval ends at p(i)-1. For a different row j, if we also want left corner c_j = (j, p(j)-1) to be in the tile, we need b = p(j)-1 as well. So p(i)-1 = p(j)-1 => p(i) = p(j). Since p is a permutation, that can't happen unless i=j. So a tile cannot contain two left corners, regardless of being left or mixed. So Lemma 1 still holds even if tile is mixed. However, the classification of tiles as left or right is used to argue that each left corner must be covered by a left tile (since right tiles cannot contain left corners). Is that true? Could a mixed tile (which is left for some rows and right for others) contain a left corner? Let's see: For a left corner c_i = (i, p(i)-1), we need tile's column interval includes column p(i)-1. If tile is mixed, it might be left for row i (i.e., b < p(i)). Then the tile's column interval must be left of p(i) for row i, which is consistent with containing left corner. So a mixed tile could be left for row i, thus could contain left corner c_i. But the solution's classification says left corners must be covered by left tiles; they define left tiles as those entirely in ∪ L_i. But a mixed tile that is left for row i but right for some other rows would have some squares in R_j for other rows j, i.e., not entirely in ∪ L_i. So it would not be a left tile per their definition. However, such a mixed tile could still contain left corners. So the claim \"Since each left-corner must be covered by some left tile (a right tile cannot contain a cell left of its row’s uncovered square), Lemma 1 implies that at least n-1 left tiles are necessary.\" This claim is flawed because a mixed tile could cover left corners while also covering squares from the right side of other rows. The argument that left corners must be covered by left tiles is not justified.\n\nThus the lower bound may be too high. The solution's lower bound argument might be invalid because they incorrectly assumed each tile is either left or right exclusively. If a tile can be mixed, then the lower bound of at least n-1 left tiles and n-1 right tiles may not hold. However, we need to check if the lower bound can be improved by a different argument. The solution's lower bound is based on counting left corners and right corners, each tile can cover at most one left corner and at most one right corner. If a tile can cover both a left corner and a right corner (i.e., a mixed tile), then it could reduce the total number of tiles needed. But can a tile cover both a left corner and a right corner? Let's examine: A left corner is at (i, p(i)-1). A right corner is at (j, p(j)+1). For a tile to contain both, it must have column interval [a,b] covering both p(i)-1 and p(j)+1. For row i, tile must be left of p(i) (b < p(i)). For row j, tile must be right of p(j) (a > p(j)). So we need a > p(j) and b < p(i). Since a <= b, we need p(j) < a <= b < p(i). So p(j) < p(i). So it's possible if p(j) < p(i) and the tile's column interval lies between them. So a tile could contain a left corner from a row with a larger p(i) and a right corner from a row with a smaller p(j). So a mixed tile could cover one left corner and one right corner, thus reducing the total number of tiles needed.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 may not be tight. The solution's construction uses only left and right tiles (no mixed tiles). But perhaps there exists a construction with fewer than 2n-2 tiles using mixed tiles. The solution's lower bound argument fails to rule out mixed tiles. So the optimality is questionable.\n\nWe need to check if the lower bound can be improved. Let's try to think about the problem: We have n rows and n columns, each row and column must have exactly one uncovered square. So we have a permutation matrix of uncovered squares. We need to tile all other squares with rectangles that do not overlap and do not include uncovered squares. We want to minimize number of tiles.\n\nThe solution uses a simple construction: For each row, tile the left part and right part separately, giving at most 2 tiles per row, but one row has no left part, one row has no right part, so total 2n-2. Could we do better? Possibly by merging left parts of multiple rows into a single tile, or merging right parts across rows, or using mixed tiles that combine left part of some rows with right part of others.\n\nBut there is also a column constraint: each column must have exactly one uncovered square. So the uncovered squares form a permutation. The remaining squares form a shape: all squares except the permutation entries. This shape is a \"grid with a diagonal removed\" but not necessarily diagonal; it's a permutation matrix removed. The shape is a set of cells that can be partitioned into rectangles. The goal is to partition the shape into as few rectangles as possible.\n\nThis is reminiscent of the problem of covering a board with rectangles after removing a permutation matrix. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n (or maybe 2n-2?). Let's think: The complement of a permutation matrix is a Ferrers board shape? Actually, if we think of the permutation matrix as a set of points, the complement is the board minus those points. We can partition the complement into monotone regions. There is known result: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into n monotone (non-intersecting) rectangles? Not sure.\n\nLet's try to find a lower bound: Each tile is a rectangle that cannot contain any uncovered square. So each tile must be contained in a region that avoids the permutation points. The complement shape is a union of \"staircase\" shapes. The minimal number of rectangles required to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n (the number of rows) maybe? Actually, we can partition the complement into n rectangles by taking each row's left part and right part as separate rectangles, but that yields up to 2n-2 rectangles. But maybe we can combine left parts of multiple rows into a single rectangle if they align in columns. For example, suppose we have two rows i and j with p(i) > p(j). Then the left part of row i includes columns 1..p(i)-1, and left part of row j includes columns 1..p(j)-1. The left part of row i includes the left part of row j plus extra columns. So we could possibly combine the left part of row j with the right part of row i? Not sure.\n\nWe need to think about the structure: The uncovered squares are at positions (i, p(i)). The complement shape can be described as the union of cells (i, j) where j < p(i) (left side) or j > p(i) (right side). So each row is split into two intervals: [1, p(i)-1] and [p(i)+1, n]. The left intervals across rows may have different lengths. The set of left intervals forms a Young diagram shape when sorted by p(i). Similarly the right intervals form another shape.\n\nWe can consider the left intervals: For each row i, we have a left interval of length p(i)-1. If we sort rows by p(i) (i.e., by the column of the uncovered square), the left intervals are nested: If p(i) < p(j), then left interval of row i is shorter than that of row j. Actually, if p(i) < p(j), then p(i)-1 < p(j)-1, so left interval of row i is a subset of left interval of row j (since both start at column 1). So the left intervals are nested: the row with the smallest p(i) has the smallest left interval; the row with the largest p(i) has the largest left interval (maybe up to n-1). So the left intervals form a chain under inclusion. Similarly, the right intervals are nested in the opposite direction: For rows i and j, if p(i) < p(j), then right interval of row i is columns p(i)+1..n, which is larger (i.e., includes more columns) than the right interval of row j (p(j)+1..n). So the right intervals are nested in reverse.\n\nThus the shape of left intervals is like a staircase shape: The union of left intervals across all rows is a Young diagram shape that is monotone: each row's left interval is a prefix of the columns, and the lengths are increasing as we go down rows sorted by p(i). So the shape is a Ferrers diagram. Similarly, the right intervals form a shape that is a suffix shape.\n\nNow, we want to partition the union of left intervals and right intervals into rectangles. Since the left intervals are nested, we can partition them into n-1 rectangles by taking each \"layer\" of the shape: For each column j (1 <= j <= n-1), the set of rows i such that p(i) > j (i.e., left interval includes column j) forms a contiguous block of rows (maybe not contiguous in original order but contiguous after sorting). Actually, if we sort rows by p(i), the rows with p(i) > j are those rows with p(i) > j, which form a suffix of the sorted order. So the shape of left intervals is a Young diagram that can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles each of width 1 (vertical strips). But we can also partition it into fewer rectangles by grouping columns that have the same set of rows. Since the shape is monotone, the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram is equal to the number of \"corners\" of the diagram, which is the number of distinct row lengths. In this case, the left intervals have lengths p(i)-1 for each i. Since p is a permutation, the lengths p(i)-1 are all distinct values from 0 to n-1. So there are n distinct lengths (including zero). The number of distinct lengths >0 is n-1. So the shape has n-1 \"steps\". The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram with distinct row lengths is n-1 (each row length is unique). Indeed, the shape is a \"staircase\" shape: row lengths are 0,1,2,...,n-1 (maybe in some order). The minimal number of rectangles to partition a staircase shape is n-1, because each row length is unique, and you cannot combine rows with different lengths into a single rectangle without covering a missing cell. Actually, you could combine rows with same length, but there are none. So you need at least n-1 rectangles for left side. Similarly, the right side also needs at least n-1 rectangles. So total at least 2n-2 rectangles. However, this argument is not exactly what the solution gave, but it's a more rigorous argument. Let's examine: The left intervals are nested, but the shape is a staircase shape (like a Young diagram with distinct row lengths). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition such a shape is indeed n-1, because each rectangle can cover a contiguous block of rows and columns. In a staircase shape, you can cover each \"step\" with a rectangle that spans the rows that have that step and the columns of that step. But perhaps you can combine steps: For example, consider rows with lengths 1 and 2: you could have a rectangle covering columns 1..1 for rows with length >=1, and a rectangle covering columns 2..2 for rows with length >=2. That's two rectangles. In general, you need one rectangle per column (except maybe the largest column). So n-1 rectangles. So the left side needs at least n-1 rectangles. Similarly, right side needs at least n-1 rectangles. So total at least 2n-2.\n\nBut is there a possibility to use a rectangle that covers both left and right sides of different rows? That would be a mixed tile. Could a mixed tile reduce the total count below 2n-2? Let's consider a mixed tile that covers left side of some rows and right side of other rows. The shape of the complement is the union of left and right intervals. The left intervals are a Young diagram shape occupying the left side of the board, and the right intervals are a Young diagram shape occupying the right side. These two shapes are disjoint (they do not intersect because they are separated by the uncovered squares). So the complement shape is the union of two disjoint shapes: left shape and right shape. A rectangle cannot intersect both shapes unless it also includes the uncovered squares in between, which is not allowed. However, a rectangle could be placed in a region that includes cells from left shape for some rows and cells from right shape for other rows, but then the rectangle would have to cross the column of the uncovered square for those rows where it changes side. But the rectangle's column interval is fixed across all rows. So if we have a rectangle that for some rows is left of the uncovered square and for other rows is right of the uncovered square, then the column interval must lie between the uncovered squares of those rows as we described earlier. That interval would be a gap between the p-values of those rows. However, that interval would be empty of any uncovered squares for those rows, but it would be located somewhere between columns p(j) and p(i). But would that interval be part of the left shape or right shape? Actually, the left shape includes columns less than p(i) for each row i. The right shape includes columns greater than p(i) for each row i. So the columns between p(j) and p(i) (where p(j) < p(i)) are left of p(i) and right of p(j). For row i, those columns are part of its left shape; for row j, those columns are part of its right shape. So the rectangle covering those columns for rows i and j would be covering left side of row i and right side of row j. So it would be partially in left shape and partially in right shape. However, the left shape and right shape are disjoint as sets of cells: left shape includes cells (i, j) where j < p(i). Right shape includes cells (i, j) where j > p(i). So the rectangle covering columns between p(j) and p(i) for rows i and j would be partially in left shape (for row i) and partially in right shape (for row j). So the rectangle would be a union of cells from left shape and right shape, but since the rectangle is contiguous, it would also include cells for any intermediate rows k between i and j (if the rectangle covers a contiguous block of rows). For those rows, the rectangle's column interval might be partially left of p(k) and partially right of p(k). But if the column interval lies between p(j) and p(i), then for any row k with p(k) between p(j) and p(i), the rectangle's column interval would intersect the uncovered square (since p(k) is inside the interval). So the rectangle cannot include such rows. So the rectangle can only cover rows whose p-values are either all > b (so tile left) or all < a (tile right) or a set of rows with p-values all either > b or < a, but not both. Actually, if we want a rectangle that covers rows i and j where p(i) > b and p(j) < a, we need to ensure that for any row k between i and j (in terms of row index, not sorted by p), the rectangle also covers that row because the rectangle's row interval is contiguous. If that row's p(k) lies between a and b, then the rectangle would contain the uncovered square (k, p(k)), which is not allowed. So the rectangle cannot include any row whose p(k) lies between a and b. So the rectangle's row interval must be a set of rows such that the p-values of those rows are either all > b or all < a. That means the rectangle cannot have rows with p-values on both sides of the interval. So the rectangle cannot be \"mixed\" in the sense of covering both left and right sides of different rows unless the rows are not contiguous in the row ordering. But the rectangle's rows must be contiguous (since it's a rectangle). So if we want to include rows i and j with p(i) > b and p(j) < a, we need to also include all rows between i and j in the row index order. If any of those rows have p-values between a and b, the rectangle would be invalid. So to avoid that, we need a block of rows where all p-values are either > b or < a. That means the rectangle is either left for all rows in the block or right for all rows in the block. So a rectangle cannot be mixed across rows unless the rows are arranged such that the p-values are monotonic with respect to row index. But p is a permutation; there is no guarantee that p-values are monotonic in row index. However, we could reorder rows? No, rows are fixed; we cannot reorder them. So a rectangle's row interval is a contiguous set of rows in the original ordering (1..n). The permutation p is arbitrary. So it's possible that there is a contiguous block of rows where the p-values are all > b (so tile left) or all < a (tile right). But if we want a rectangle that is left for some rows and right for others, we would need a block of rows where p-values are all > b for some rows and all < a for others, which would require p-values to be either all > b or all < a, but not both. So a rectangle cannot be left for some rows and right for others within the same block. Therefore, the classification that each tile is either left tile or right tile might actually be correct, because the rectangle's row interval must be contiguous, and the p-values across that interval cannot straddle the interval [a,b] (the column interval). Let's analyze more carefully.\n\nWe have a rectangle T with rows from r1 to r2 (inclusive) and columns from a to b (inclusive). For each row i in [r1, r2], we need either b < p(i) (tile left) or a > p(i) (tile right). If for some rows i we have b < p(i) (left) and for some other rows j we have a > p(j) (right), then we have p(i) > b >= a > p(j). So p(i) > p(j). That's fine. However, we also need that for any row k between i and j (in terms of row index), the condition holds. If the rows are not sorted by p, there could be a row k with p(k) between a and b, which would be invalid. So for the rectangle to be valid, the set of rows in the interval must be such that all p-values are either > b or < a, not a mixture. If there is any row with p(k) in [a,b], the rectangle would contain the uncovered square (k, p(k)). So the rectangle can be left for all rows in its interval (if all p(i) > b) or right for all rows (if all p(i) < a). It cannot be left for some rows and right for others unless the rows are not contiguous. But rectangles require contiguity. So indeed, each rectangle must be entirely left or entirely right of the uncovered squares for all rows it covers. Therefore the classification of each tile as left tile or right tile is correct. The earlier thought about mixed tiles is invalid because of the contiguity requirement.\n\nThus the solution's claim that each tile is either left or right is valid. Let's double-check: Suppose we have a rectangle covering rows i and i+1 (two consecutive rows). Suppose p(i) = 5, p(i+1) = 2. Suppose we choose column interval [3,4]. Then for row i, b=4 < p(i)=5, so tile left for row i. For row i+1, a=3 > p(i+1)=2, so tile right for row i+1. So the rectangle is left for row i and right for row i+1. However, does it contain any uncovered squares? For row i, uncovered square is at column 5, not in [3,4]. For row i+1, uncovered square is at column 2, not in [3,4]. So the rectangle does not contain any uncovered squares. So this rectangle is valid, and it is \"mixed\". But does it contain any left corners or right corners? Let's see: left corner for row i is at column p(i)-1 = 4, which is in the rectangle (since rectangle includes column 4). So left corner c_i = (i,4) is in the rectangle. Right corner for row i+1 is at column p(i+1)+1 = 3, which is also in the rectangle (since rectangle includes column 3). So rectangle contains both a left corner and a right corner. So the rectangle is mixed and contains both a left corner and a right corner. This seems to contradict the earlier claim that each tile is either left or right. But we need to verify if such a rectangle is allowed: The rectangle's column interval is [3,4]. For row i, p(i) = 5 > b = 4, so left. For row i+1, p(i+1) = 2 < a = 3, so right. So the rectangle does not contain any uncovered squares. So it is a valid tile. So the classification that each tile is either left or right is false. The rectangle can be left for some rows and right for others, as long as the column interval lies between the p-values of those rows. The contiguity of rows does not prevent this, as shown by the example. Indeed, we have a rectangle covering rows i and i+1, with column interval [3,4], which is between p(i+1)=2 and p(i)=5. So the rectangle is valid. So the solution's claim that each tile must be either left tile or right tile is false. However, does the solution rely on this claim for the lower bound? Yes, they use the claim to argue that each left corner must be covered by a left tile, and each right corner must be covered by a right tile. But as we just saw, a mixed tile could cover a left corner (for a row where it's left) and also a right corner (for another row). So left corners could be covered by a tile that is not a left tile (i.e., a mixed tile). So the lower bound argument that there must be at least n-1 left tiles is flawed.\n\nBut perhaps they also rely on the fact that a left tile can contain at most one left corner, and a right tile can contain at most one right corner. If a mixed tile can contain at most one left corner and at most one right corner, then the total number of left corners (n-1) plus right corners (n-1) could be covered by at most n-1 left tiles + n-1 right tiles, but maybe a mixed tile could cover both a left corner and a right corner, reducing the total number of tiles needed. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may not hold.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound is not rigorous. However, we need to check if there is any known result that the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is indeed 2n-2. Let's think about known results: The problem of covering a board with rectangles avoiding a set of points is reminiscent of the concept of \"rectangular partition of a grid with a permutation matrix removed\". The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n. Actually, I recall a known combinatorial result: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into n monotone (non-intersecting) rectangles, each covering a \"hook\" shape? Not sure. Let's try to find a small example to test: n=2. The board is 2x2. Uncovered squares must be one per row and column, so they are a permutation matrix: either uncovered squares at (1,1) and (2,2) or at (1,2) and (2,1). Let's consider p = identity: uncovered squares at (1,1) and (2,2). Then the remaining squares are (1,2) and (2,1). These are two squares not in the same row or column. They cannot be covered by a single rectangle because a rectangle covering both would also include (1,1) and (2,2) (the uncovered squares). So we need two tiles: one covering (1,2) and one covering (2,1). So minimum number of tiles is 2 = 2n-2 = 2*2-2 = 2. So for n=2, the bound is tight.\n\nn=3: Let's test a permutation p = (1,2,3) identity: uncovered squares at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). The remaining squares are all off-diagonal cells. The shape is the board minus the diagonal. Can we tile this with fewer than 2n-2 = 4 tiles? Let's try to tile with 3 tiles. Is it possible? Let's attempt to find a tiling: The board is 3x3. Uncovered squares: (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). The remaining squares: (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). Let's try to tile them with rectangles that avoid the diagonal squares. Could we use a rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3) (row 1, columns 2-3) - that's a tile. Another rectangle covering (2,1) (just a 1x1 tile) maybe. Another rectangle covering (2,3),(3,3?) No, (3,3) is uncovered. Could we use a rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1) (column 1 rows 2-3) - that's a tile. Then we have left (2,3),(3,2). Could we cover those with a rectangle? They are at (2,3) and (3,2). They are not aligned in a rectangle without covering (2,2) or (3,3) which are uncovered. So we need separate tiles for each: (2,3) and (3,2) each need a tile. So total tiles: (1,2)-(1,3) tile, (2,1)-(3,1) tile, (2,3) tile, (3,2) tile = 4 tiles. So 4 is needed. Could we do a different arrangement? Maybe use a rectangle covering (1,2),(2,2?) No, (2,2) is uncovered. So cannot. So seems 4 is minimal. So 2n-2 = 4 works.\n\nn=3 with a different permutation: p = (1,3,2) i.e., uncovered squares at (1,1), (2,3), (3,2). Let's see if we can tile with fewer than 4. The board: Row1: uncovered at col1, left part empty, right part columns 2-3. Row2: uncovered at col3, left part columns 1-2, right part empty. Row3: uncovered at col2, left part column 1, right part column 3. So left corners: row2 left corner at (2,2), row3 left corner at (3,1). Right corners: row1 right corner at (1,2), row3 right corner at (3,3). So left corners count = 2, right corners count = 2. The solution's lower bound says at least 4 tiles. Could we tile with 3? Let's try: Use a rectangle covering row1 columns 2-3 (covers (1,2),(1,3)). That's a tile. Use a rectangle covering row2 columns 1-2 (covers (2,1),(2,2)). That's a tile. Use a rectangle covering row3 column 3 (covers (3,3)). That's a tile. But we also need to cover (3,1) and (3,? Actually row3 left part is column 1, right part is column 3. So (3,1) is uncovered? No, uncovered at (3,2). So (3,1) is left part, (3,3) is right part. We covered (3,3) with tile, but (3,1) is not covered. So we need a tile for (3,1). That would be a 1x1 tile. So total 4 tiles. Could we combine (3,1) with something else? Maybe combine (3,1) with (2,1) and (2,2) etc. But (2,1)-(2,2) tile already covers (2,1) and (2,2). Could we extend that tile to include row3 column1? That would be a rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 1-2? That would include (3,2) which is uncovered. So not allowed. Could we make a rectangle covering rows 2-3, column 1 only? That would be a tile covering (2,1) and (3,1). But (2,1) is already covered by the tile covering row2 columns 1-2. But we could adjust: Instead of tile covering row2 columns 1-2, we could have a tile covering rows 2-3, column 1 (cover (2,1),(3,1)). Then we need to cover (2,2) (left part of row2). (2,2) is left corner. Could we cover (2,2) with a tile that also covers (1,2) maybe? Let's see: (1,2) is right corner for row1. Could we have a rectangle covering rows 1-2, column 2? That would be (1,2),(2,2). Does it contain any uncovered squares? Row1 uncovered at col1, not in column2. Row2 uncovered at col3, not in column2. So it's allowed. So we could have tile covering column2 rows 1-2. Then we need to cover (1,3) and (3,3). Could we have a tile covering rows 1 and 3, column 3? That would be rows 1-3 (including row2) column3, but row2 column3 is uncovered (since uncovered at (2,3)). So not allowed. Could we have tile covering (1,3) alone and (3,3) alone? That's two more tiles. So total tiles: tile covering rows2-3 col1 (2 tiles), tile covering rows1-2 col2 (1 tile), tile covering (1,3) (1 tile), tile covering (3,3) (1 tile) = 5 tiles. That's worse.\n\nThus seems 4 tiles is minimal for n=3. So 2n-2 holds for n=3.\n\nn=4: Let's test if we can do less than 2n-2 = 6. Perhaps we can do 5? Let's try to find a permutation and a tiling with 5 rectangles. Let's see if it's possible. The shape is more complex. But maybe the lower bound is indeed 2n-2. However, we need to verify the general case.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument is flawed in that it incorrectly claims each left corner must be covered by a left tile. However, maybe there is a stronger argument that each left corner must be covered by a tile that is left for that row, but that tile could be a mixed tile that is left for that row and right for other rows. But the left corner is still covered by a tile (maybe mixed). So the lower bound on number of left tiles may not hold, but the lower bound on total number of tiles may still hold if we consider that each tile can cover at most one left corner and at most one right corner. If each tile can cover at most one left corner and at most one right corner, then we need at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1 tiles to cover left corners, and at least n-1 tiles to cover right corners. However, a tile could cover both a left corner and a right corner, thus potentially covering two corners with one tile. So the total number of tiles needed could be less than 2n-2. For example, if we pair each left corner with a right corner in a mixed tile, we could reduce the total number of tiles to n-1 (if we could pair all left corners with right corners). But can we pair them all? There are n-1 left corners and n-1 right corners. If we could pair each left corner with a distinct right corner in a tile, we would need at most n-1 tiles. But is that possible? Let's think: For each left corner c_i = (i, p(i)-1), we need a tile that includes column p(i)-1. For a right corner d_j = (j, p(j)+1), we need a tile that includes column p(j)+1. For a tile to contain both c_i and d_j, the column interval must include both p(i)-1 and p(j)+1. So the interval must be at least from min(p(i)-1, p(j)+1) to max(p(i)-1, p(j)+1). Since p(i)-1 <= p(i)-1 and p(j)+1 >= p(j)+1. For the tile to be valid, for row i, we need b < p(i) (since tile includes column p(i)-1, which is less than p(i), so b must be >= p(i)-1 but < p(i)). So b = p(i)-1. For row j, we need a > p(j) (since tile includes column p(j)+1, which is > p(j), so a <= p(j)+1 but > p(j)). So a = p(j)+1. So the column interval must be exactly [p(j)+1, p(i)-1] (assuming p(j)+1 <= p(i)-1). This requires p(j)+1 <= p(i)-1 => p(j) + 2 <= p(i). So p(i) - p(j) >= 2. So we need a pair of rows i, j such that p(i) >= p(j) + 2. Then we can have a tile covering left corner of row i and right corner of row j. The tile's column interval is [p(j)+1, p(i)-1]. For the tile to be valid for both rows, we need that for any row k in the row interval (contiguous rows), p(k) is either > b (i.e., > p(i)-1) or < a (i.e., < p(j)+1). If there is any row k with p(k) between a and b, the tile would contain uncovered square (k, p(k)). So we need to ensure that the set of rows covered by the tile have p-values all either > p(i)-1 (i.e., > p(i)-1) or < p(j)+1. Since p(i)-1 is the maximum column in the interval, any row with p(k) > p(i)-1 would be left of the tile (b < p(k)), which is okay. Any row with p(k) < p(j)+1 would be right of the tile (a > p(k)), also okay. But rows with p(k) between p(j)+1 and p(i)-1 inclusive would cause a problem. So the tile can only cover rows whose p-values are either all > p(i)-1 or all < p(j)+1. Since we have rows i and j with p(i) > p(j)+1, it's possible that there are other rows with p-values between p(j)+1 and p(i)-1. If we include those rows in the tile's row interval, we would have a problem. So the tile's row interval must be a contiguous block that includes rows i and j but excludes any rows with p-values in that interval. That may be impossible if the rows i and j are not adjacent in the row ordering and there are rows between them with p-values in the interval. However, we could choose a tile that only covers rows i and j, but the rows must be contiguous. If i and j are not adjacent, we cannot have a rectangle covering only those two rows without also covering the rows in between. So we need i and j to be consecutive rows (or the rows between them have p-values outside the interval). So it's not generally possible to pair all left and right corners into mixed tiles.\n\nThus the lower bound may be more subtle.\n\nNevertheless, the solution's lower bound argument is flawed because it incorrectly asserts that left corners must be covered by left tiles. However, the lower bound might still hold via a more rigorous argument: each left corner must be covered by a tile that is left for that row; that tile could be mixed, but it's still left for that row. The lemma says a left tile can contain at most one left corner. But a mixed tile that is left for that row is not a left tile per the solution's definition. However, the lemma's proof only uses the fact that if a tile contains a left corner, then its rightmost column b = p(i)-1. So any tile containing a left corner must have b = p(i)-1. Could a mixed tile contain two left corners? If it contains left corners for rows i and j, then we need b = p(i)-1 and also b = p(j)-1, so p(i) = p(j), impossible. So any tile can contain at most one left corner, regardless of being left or mixed. So the bound that there are n-1 left corners, each tile can contain at most one left corner, implies at least n-1 tiles are needed to cover all left corners. This does not require the tile to be left; any tile covering a left corner is counted. So the lower bound is that at least n-1 tiles are needed to