ner at (3,1). Right corners: row1 right corner at (1,2), row3 right corner at (3,3). So left corners count = 2, right corners count = 2. The solution's lower bound says at least 4 tiles. Could we tile with 3? Let's try: Use a rectangle covering row1 columns 2-3 (covers (1,2),(1,3)). That's a tile. Use a rectangle covering row2 columns 1-2 (covers (2,1),(2,2)). That's a tile. Use a rectangle covering row3 column 3 (covers (3,3)). That's a tile. But we also need to cover (3,1) and (3,? Actually row3 left part is column 1, right part is column 3. So (3,1) is uncovered? No, uncovered at (3,2). So (3,1) is left part, (3,3) is right part. We covered (3,3) with tile, but (3,1) is not covered. So we need a tile for (3,1). That would be a 1x1 tile. So total 4 tiles. Could we combine (3,1) with something else? Maybe combine (3,1) with (2,1) and (2,2) etc. But (2,1)-(2,2) tile already covers (2,1) and (2,2). Could we extend that tile to include row3 column1? That would be a rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 1-2? That would include (3,2) which is uncovered. So not allowed. Could we make a rectangle covering rows 2-3, column 1 only? That would be a tile covering (2,1) and (3,1). But (2,1) is already covered by the tile covering row2 columns 1-2. But we could adjust: Instead of tile covering row2 columns 1-2, we could have a tile covering rows 2-3, column 1 (cover (2,1),(3,1)). Then we need to cover (2,2) (left part of row2). (2,2) is left corner. Could we cover (2,2) with a tile that also covers (1,2) maybe? Let's see: (1,2) is right corner for row1. Could we have a rectangle covering rows 1-2, column 2? That would be (1,2),(2,2). Does it contain any uncovered squares? Row1 uncovered at col1, not in column2. Row2 uncovered at col3, not in column2. So it's allowed. So we could have tile covering column2 rows 1-2. Then we need to cover (1,3) and (3,3). Could we have a tile covering rows 1 and 3, column 3? That would be rows 1-3 (including row2) column3, but row2 column3 is uncovered (since uncovered at (2,3)). So not allowed. Could we have tile covering (1,3) alone and (3,3) alone? That's two more tiles. So total tiles: tile covering rows2-3 col1 (2 tiles), tile covering rows1-2 col2 (1 tile), tile covering (1,3) (1 tile), tile covering (3,3) (1 tile) = 5 tiles. That's worse.\n\nThus seems 4 tiles is minimal for n=3. So 2n-2 holds for n=3.\n\nn=4: Let's test if we can do less than 2n-2 = 6. Perhaps we can do 5? Let's try to find a permutation and a tiling with 5 rectangles. Let's see if it's possible. The shape is more complex. But maybe the lower bound is indeed 2n-2. However, we need to verify the general case.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument is flawed in that it incorrectly claims each left corner must be covered by a left tile. However, maybe there is a stronger argument that each left corner must be covered by a tile that is left for that row, but that tile could be a mixed tile that is left for that row and right for other rows. But the left corner is still covered by a tile (maybe mixed). So the lower bound on number of left tiles may not hold, but the lower bound on total number of tiles may still hold if we consider that each tile can cover at most one left corner and at most one right corner. If each tile can cover at most one left corner and at most one right corner, then we need at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1 tiles to cover left corners, and at least n-1 tiles to cover right corners. However, a tile could cover both a left corner and a right corner, thus potentially covering two corners with one tile. So the total number of tiles needed could be less than 2n-2. For example, if we pair each left corner with a right corner in a mixed tile, we could reduce the total number of tiles to n-1 (if we could pair all left corners with right corners). But can we pair them all? There are n-1 left corners and n-1 right corners. If we could pair each left corner with a distinct right corner in a tile, we would need at most n-1 tiles. But is that possible? Let's think: For each left corner c_i = (i, p(i)-1), we need a tile that includes column p(i)-1. For a right corner d_j = (j, p(j)+1), we need a tile that includes column p(j)+1. For a tile to contain both c_i and d_j, the column interval must include both p(i)-1 and p(j)+1. So the interval must be at least from min(p(i)-1, p(j)+1) to max(p(i)-1, p(j)+1). Since p(i)-1 <= p(i)-1 and p(j)+1 >= p(j)+1. For the tile to be valid, for row i, we need b < p(i) (since tile includes column p(i)-1, which is less than p(i), so b must be >= p(i)-1 but < p(i)). So b = p(i)-1. For row j, we need a > p(j) (since tile includes column p(j)+1, which is > p(j), so a <= p(j)+1 but > p(j)). So a = p(j)+1. So the column interval must be exactly [p(j)+1, p(i)-1] (assuming p(j)+1 <= p(i)-1). This requires p(j)+1 <= p(i)-1 => p(j) + 2 <= p(i). So p(i) - p(j) >= 2. So we need a pair of rows i, j such that p(i) >= p(j) + 2. Then we can have a tile covering left corner of row i and right corner of row j. The tile's column interval is [p(j)+1, p(i)-1]. For the tile to be valid for both rows, we need that for any row k in the row interval (contiguous rows), p(k) is either > b (i.e., > p(i)-1) or < a (i.e., < p(j)+1). If there is any row k with p(k) between a and b, the tile would contain uncovered square (k, p(k)). So we need to ensure that the set of rows covered by the tile have p-values all either > p(i)-1 (i.e., > p(i)-1) or < p(j)+1. Since p(i)-1 is the maximum column in the interval, any row with p(k) > p(i)-1 would be left of the tile (b < p(k)), which is okay. Any row with p(k) < p(j)+1 would be right of the tile (a > p(k)), also okay. But rows with p(k) between p(j)+1 and p(i)-1 inclusive would cause a problem. So the tile can only cover rows whose p-values are either all > p(i)-1 or all < p(j)+1. Since we have rows i and j with p(i) > p(j)+1, it's possible that there are other rows with p-values between p(j)+1 and p(i)-1. If we include those rows in the tile's row interval, we would have a problem. So the tile's row interval must be a contiguous block that includes rows i and j but excludes any rows with p-values in that interval. That may be impossible if the rows i and j are not adjacent in the row ordering and there are rows between them with p-values in the interval. However, we could choose a tile that only covers rows i and j, but the rows must be contiguous. If i and j are not adjacent, we cannot have a rectangle covering only those two rows without also covering the rows in between. So we need i and j to be consecutive rows (or the rows between them have p-values outside the interval). So it's not generally possible to pair all left and right corners into mixed tiles.\n\nThus the lower bound may be more subtle.\n\nNevertheless, the solution's lower bound argument is flawed because it incorrectly asserts that left corners must be covered by left tiles. However, the lower bound might still hold via a more rigorous argument: each left corner must be covered by a tile that is left for that row; that tile could be mixed, but it's still left for that row. The lemma says a left tile can contain at most one left corner. But a mixed tile that is left for that row is not a left tile per the solution's definition. However, the lemma's proof only uses the fact that if a tile contains a left corner, then its rightmost column b = p(i)-1. So any tile containing a left corner must have b = p(i)-1. Could a mixed tile contain two left corners? If it contains left corners for rows i and j, then we need b = p(i)-1 and also b = p(j)-1, so p(i) = p(j), impossible. So any tile can contain at most one left corner, regardless of being left or mixed. So the bound that there are n-1 left corners, each tile can contain at most one left corner, implies at least n-1 tiles are needed to cover all left corners. This does not require the tile to be left; any tile covering a left corner is counted. So the lower bound is that at least n-1 tiles are needed to cover left corners. Similarly, at least n-1 tiles are needed to cover right corners. However, a tile could cover both a left corner and a right corner, thus counting for both lower bounds simultaneously. So the total number of tiles needed is at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1, but we need to consider that each tile can cover at most one left corner and at most one right corner, but could cover both. So the total number of tiles needed is at least ceil(( (n-1)+(n-1) ) / 2 ) = n-1? Actually, if each tile can cover at most one left corner and at most one right corner, the total number of tiles needed to cover all left and right corners is at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1, but also at least ceil((2(n-1))/2) = n-1. So the lower bound could be as low as n-1, not 2n-2. So the solution's lower bound is too high.\n\nBut maybe there is an additional constraint that a tile cannot contain both a left corner and a right corner. Let's examine: Suppose a tile contains a left corner c_i = (i, p(i)-1). Then b = p(i)-1. For the tile to also contain a right corner d_j = (j, p(j)+1), we need a <= p(j)+1 <= b. Since b = p(i)-1, we need p(j)+1 <= p(i)-1 => p(j) <= p(i)-2. So p(j) < p(i)-1. So p(j) < p(i). So the tile's column interval must be [a,b] where a <= p(j)+1 <= b = p(i)-1. So a <= p(j)+1. Also a > p(j) (since tile must be right of uncovered square for row j). So a = p(j)+1. So the column interval is exactly [p(j)+1, p(i)-1]. So the tile's column interval is non-empty only if p(i) - p(j) >= 2. So we need p(i) >= p(j)+2. So it's possible.\n\nNow, does the tile's row interval include both rows i and j? Yes, we need the tile to cover rows i and j. The tile's row interval must be contiguous. So if i and j are not adjacent, we need to include rows between them. Those rows may have p-values that fall within the column interval, causing uncovered squares to be included. So we need to ensure that for any row k between i and j (in row index order), p(k) is either > p(i)-1 (so left of tile) or