t\\n\\\\[\\nT = \\\\{(i+1,i)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1\\\\}\\\\subset L.\\n\\\\]\\nAgain each tile lying in $L$ can contain at most one element of $T$, so at least $n-1$ tiles are needed for $L$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**5.  Combined lower bound**\\n\\nTiles covering $U$ and tiles covering $L$ are disjoint, therefore any admissible tiling uses at least\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**6.  Construction attaining the bound**\\n\\nPlace the uncovered squares on the diagonal $D$ as described in step 1.  For each $i=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ define two tiles:\\n\\n* Horizontal tile\\n  \\\\[H_i = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i+1\\\\le j\\\\le n\\\\},\\\\]\\n  which is a $1\\\\times (n-i)$ rectangle.\\n* Vertical tile\\n  \\\\[V_i = \\\\{(j,i)\\\\mid i+1\\\\le j\\\\le n\\\\},\\\\]\\n  which is a $(n-i)\\\\times 1$ rectangle.\\n\\nThe family\\n\\\\[\\\\mathcal{T}=\\\\{H_i,V_i\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1\\\\}\\\\]\\ncontains exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  They are pairwise disjoint because each $H_i$ occupies only row $i$, while each $V_i$ occupies only column $i$, and the row and column indices differ for distinct $i$.  Moreover no tile contains a diagonal cell: $H_i$ uses columns $>i$, and $V_i$ uses rows $>i$.\\n\\nEvery non‑diagonal square belongs to exactly one tile:\\n* If a square $(i,j)$ satisfies $i<j$, then it lies in $H_i$ (same row $i$, column $j\\\\ge i+1$).\\n* If $i>j$, then it lies in $V_j$ (same column $j$, row $i\\\\ge j+1$).\\nThus the $2n-2$ tiles cover all $n^2-n$ squares that are not on $D$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**7.  Minimality**\\n\\nStep 5 shows that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, while Step 6 provides a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n-------------------------------