he unique uncovered square $(i,i)$.  Any admissible tiling for this choice of $U$ will give a lower bound that applies to all choices, because the problem asks for the *minimum* over all possible placements of the uncovered squares.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**2.  Geometry of a single tile**\\n\\nA tile is a rectangle $R=I\\\\times J$, where $I$ is a set of consecutive rows and $J$ a set of consecutive columns.  Since $R$ must avoid the diagonal squares, it cannot contain points both with row index $\\\\le$ column index and with row index $\\\\ge$ column index.  Consequently every tile lies entirely in one of the two strict triangular regions:\\n\\\\[\\nU = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i<j\\\\}\\\\qquad\\\\text{(strictly above the diagonal)}\\n\\\\]\\nor\\n\\\\[\\nL = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i>j\\\\}\\\\qquad\\\\text{(strictly below the diagonal)}.\\n\\\\]\\nA tile that mixes squares from $U$ and $L$ would have to cross a diagonal cell, which is forbidden.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**3.  A lower bound for the upper region**\\n\\nConsider the $n-1$ cells\\n\\\\[\\nS = \\\\{(i,i+1)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1\\\\}\\\\subset U.\\n\\\\]\\nThese cells lie in distinct rows and distinct columns.  Suppose a single tile $R=I\\\\times J\\\\subset U$ contains two of them, say $(i,i+1)$ and $(k,k+1)$ with $i<k$.  Then\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\max I \\\\ge k,\\\\qquad \\\\min J \\\\le i+1.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause $R\\\\subset U$, every square of $R$ satisfies row $<$ column, i.e. $\\\\max I < \\\\min J$.  But $k\\\\ge i+1$, so $\\\\max I \\\\ge \\\\min J$, a contradiction.  Hence **any tile contained in $U$ can cover at most one cell of $S$**.\\n\\nSince the $n-1$ cells of $S$ must be covered, at least $n-1$ tiles are required for the region $U$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**4.  A lower bound for the lower region**\\n\\nApply the same argument to the symmetric set\\n