2=4048$.\\n\\n**2. A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles.**\\nWe treat the left‑hand and right‑hand parts of the board separately.\\n\\n*Left part.*  For each row $i$ the cells left of the uncovered square are\\n\\\\[L_i=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le j<p(i)\\\\}.\\\\]\\nIf $p(i)>1$ then the cell $(i,\\\\,p(i)-1)$ is the **rightmost** cell of $L_i$.\\nBecause $p$ is a permutation, for every column $c$ with $1\\\\le c\\\\le n-1$ there is a unique row $i_c$ such that $p(i_c)=c+1$.\\nConsequently $(i_c,c)$ is the rightmost cell of $L_{i_c}$.\\nAny rectangle covering $(i_c,c)$ cannot extend to column $c+1$, for that column contains the uncovered square $(i_c,c+1)$.  Hence the rectangle that covers $(i_c,c)$ has rightmost column exactly $c$.\\nDistinct values of $c$ force distinct rectangles, so at least $n-1$ rectangles are required to cover the left part.\\n\\n*Right part.*  Analogously, define the right‑hand cells of row $i$ as\\n\\\\[R_i=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid p(i)<j\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]If $p(i)<n$ then $(i,\\\\,p(i)+1)$ is the **leftmost** cell of $R_i$.\\nFor each column $c$ with $2\\\\le c\\\\le n$ there is a unique row $i_c$ such that $p(i_c)=c-1$.\\nThus $(i_c,c)$ is the leftmost cell of $R_{i_c}$, and any rectangle covering it cannot extend to column $c-1$ (which contains the uncovered square $(i_c,c-1)$).  Hence the rectangle that covers $(i_c,c)$ has leftmost column exactly $c$.\\nAgain the $n-1$ different values of $c$ produce $n-1$ distinct rectangles.\\n\\nAdding the two sides, any tiling of the complement of a permutation matrix needs at least\\n\\\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\\\]\\nrectangular tiles.\\nThus\\n\\\\[F(n)\\\\ge 2n-2.\\\\]\\n\\n**3. An explicit construction attaining $2n-2$ tiles.**\\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. take $p(i)=i$ for all $i$.\\nThe uncovered squares are then $(i,i)$, $i=1,\\\\dots,n$.\\nThe remaining cells split naturally into two monotone regions:\\n\\\\[\\\\text{Left region}=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j<i\\\\},\\\\qquad\\n\\\\text{Right region}=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j>i\\\\}.\\\\]\\nFor $k=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ define the following rectangles:\\n\\\\[L_k=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid k+1\\\\le i\\\\le n,\\\\;1\\\\le j\\\\le k\\\\},\\\\]\\n\\\\[R_k=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le k,\\\\;k+1\\\\le j\\\\le n\\\\}.\\\\]\\nEach $L_k$ lies entirely in the left region, each $R_k$ lies entirely in the right region, and the families $\\\\{L_k\\\\}$ and $\\\\{R_k\\\\}$ are pairwise disjoint.\\nA straightforward verification shows that the $2n-2$ rectangles $L_1,\\\\dots,L_{n-1},R_1,\\\\dots,R_{n-1}$ cover every cell except the diagonal squares.\\nHence $f(p)=2n-2$ for this permutation, and consequently\\n\\\\[F(n)\\\\le 2n-2.\\\\]\\n\\n**4. Conclusion.**\\nFrom the lower bound and the explicit construction we obtain\\n\\\\[F(n)=2n-2\\\\]\\nfor all $n\\\\ge2$.\\nFor the concrete size $n=2025$ the minimum number of tiles is\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\\\]\\n\\nThus the answer to the problem is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\n### End of Detailed Solution ###\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
