ently the rightmost column of such a rectangle is exactly c.\n\nSuppose a single rectangle covered two distinct left\u2011critical cells (i_c , c) and (i_{c'} , c') with c < c'. Because the rectangle contains column c' its column set contains every column between its leftmost column and c', in particular column c+1. The row i_c belongs to the rectangle, so the rectangle would contain the uncovered square (i_c , c+1), a contradiction. Hence no rectangle can contain two different left\u2011critical cells. Since there are n-1 left\u2011critical cells, at least n-1 rectangles are required to cover them.\n\nDefine right\u2011critical cells analogously. For each column c with 2 \\le c \\le n let j_c be the unique row such that p(j_c) = c-1. The cell (j_c , c) lies strictly to the right of the uncovered square (j_c , c-1) in its row; we call it a right\u2011critical cell. By the same argument, a rectangle covering a right\u2011critical cell cannot extend to column c-1, so its leftmost column is exactly c, and no rectangle can contain two different right\u2011critical cells. There are again n-1 right\u2011critical cells, so at least n-1 additional rectangles are needed.\n\nThus any tiling of the complement of a permutation matrix uses at least (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 rectangles. Hence\n\nF(n) \\ge 2n-2.   (1)\n\n3. Construction attaining 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nTake the identity permutation p(i)=i for all i. The uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells (i,i), i=1,\u2026,n.\n\nFor each column c with 1 \\le c \\le n-1 define the vertical strip\n\nR_c = { (i,c) : i = c+1, c+2, \u2026 , n }.\n\nGeometrically R_c is a rectangle of width 1 and height n-c whose upper side lies on the grid line just below the diagonal cell (c,c). Because the strip contains only rows strictly larger than c, it does not contain the diagonal cell (c,c) and therefore does not contain any uncovered square.\n\nFor each row r with 1 \\le r \\le n-1 define the horizontal strip\n\nC_r = { (r,j) : j = r+1, r+2, \u2026 , n }.\n\nGeometrically C_r is a rectangle of height 1 and width n-r whose left side lies on the grid line just to the right of the diagonal cell (r,r). Since the strip contains only columns strictly larger than r, it avoids the uncovered square (r,r).\n\nThe family {R_c : 1 \\le c \\le n-1} \\cup {C_r : 1 \\le r \\le n-1} consists of exactly 2n-2 rectangles. They are pairwise disjoint: a cell with i > j belongs to the unique vertical strip R_j and to no horizontal strip, while a cell with i < j belongs to the unique horizontal strip C_i and to no vertical strip. The diagonal cells (i,i) are uncovered by construction. Consequently the 2n-2 rectangles cover every unit square except the required one per row and column.\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with 2n-2 tiles, showing that\n\nF(n) \\le 2n-2.   (2)\n\n4. Conclusion.\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain F(n) = 2n-2 for all n \\ge 2. For n = 2025 this yields\n\n2\\cdot2025 - 2 = 4048.\n\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\boxed{4048}."
