rely in $R$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left part $L$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nConsider the rows ordered by increasing $\\pi(i)$.  Define a permutation $\\sigma$ of $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ such that\n\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma(1))<\\pi(\\sigma(2))<\\dots<\\pi(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\nIf we reorder the rows of the board according to $\\sigma$, the set $L$ becomes the Ferrers diagram\n\\[\nD_L = \\{(k,j):1\\le k\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le \\pi(\\sigma(k))-1\\}.\n\\]\nBecause $\\pi(\\sigma(k))$ runs through $1,2,\\dots,n$, the row lengths of $D_L$ are\n\\[0,1,2,\\dots,n-1.\n\\]\nThus $D_L$ has $n-1$ distinct positive row lengths.\n\n**Lemma (Ferrers diagram).**  Let a Ferrers diagram have row lengths $a_1\\ge a_2\\ge\\dots\\ge a_m$.  Let $k$ be the number of distinct positive values among the $a_i$.  Any partition of the diagram into axis\u2011aligned rectangles uses at least $k$ rectangles.\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*  For each distinct row length $\\ell$ choose the leftmost cell in the row where the length jumps from $\\ell$ to a smaller value; these cells are called *corners*.  Two different corners lie in different rows and different columns, and any rectangle that contains two corners would also contain the cell at the intersection of the higher row and the smaller column, which is not present in the diagram \u2013 a contradiction.  Hence each rectangle can contain at most one corner, and there are $k$ corners, so at least $k$ rectangles are needed.  \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma to $D_L$, we obtain that any tiling of $D_L$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.\n\nNow take an arbitrary tiling of $L$ on the original board.  Apply the row permutation $\\sigma$ to the whole tiling.  A tile that was a rectangle before the permutation may become a union of several rectangles after the rows are reordered (its rows may be split into several contiguous blocks).  Splitting a tile can only increase the total number of rectangles.  Consequently the number of rectangles required for $L$ on the original board is **at least** the minimum number required for $D_L$, i.e. at least $n-1$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right part $R$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe right part is symmetric.  Reordering the rows by decreasing $\\pi(i)$ turns $R$ into the Ferrers diagram with row lengths $n-\\pi(i)$, which again are $0,1,\\dots,n-1$ in some order.  By the same lemma, any tiling of $R$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.  Hence any tiling of the whole board needs at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A construction attaining the bound\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nTake the identity permutation $\\pi(i)=i$ (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  For each column $c$ with $1\\le c\\le n-1$ place a vertical $1$\u2011wide tile covering the cells\n\\[(i,c)\\quad\\text{with }i=c+1,c+2,\\dots,n.\n\\]\nThese $n-1$ tiles cover exactly the left part $L$.\n\nFor each column $c$ with $2\\le c\\le n$ place a vertical $1$\u2011wide tile covering the cells\n\\[(i,c)\\quad\\text{with }i=1,2,\\dots,c-1.\n\\]\nThese $n-1$ tiles cover exactly the right part $R$.\n\nThe two families use disjoint sets of cells (in each column the left tile occupies the rows below the diagonal, the right tile the rows above), and together they cover every cell of the board except the $n$ diagonal squares, which are precisely the uncovered squares.  The total number of tiles is\n\\[ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this equals $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe have shown that any admissible placement of tiles requires at least $2n-2$ tiles, and we have exhibited a placement using exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** $\\boxed{4048}$.\n"
