                "content": ".\n\n{\n  \"solution\": \"### Summary ###\\n\\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\n**b. Method Sketch:**\\n- Let $n=2025$ and let $\\\\pi$ be the permutation that records, for each row $i$, the column $\\\\pi(i)$ of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  For each column $j$ let $r_j$ be the (unique) row with $\\\\pi(r_j)=j$.\\n- **Lower bound:** For every $c\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ the cell $(r_{c+1},c)$ lies to the left of its hole.  Any rectangle covering this cell must be entirely to the left of the hole in row $r_{c+1}$, hence its rightmost column is exactly $c$.  Consequently there must be at least one rectangle whose rightmost column is $c$.  Thus at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed to cover all cells that are left of the holes.\\n  By symmetry, for every $c\\\\in\\\\{2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ the cell $(r_{c-1},c)$ lies to the right of its hole, forcing a rectangle whose leftmost column is exactly $c$.  Hence at least another $n-1$ rectangles are needed for the cells that are right of the holes.\\n  Adding the two estimates gives a universal lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles.\\n- **Construction:** For each column $j$ draw a vertical rectangle that covers all squares **above** the uncovered square in that column (i.e. rows $1,\\\\dots,r_j-1$).  For each column $j$ draw a vertical rectangle that covers all squares **below** the uncovered square in that column (rows $r_j+1,\\\\dots,n$).  All these rectangles are disjoint, none contains an uncovered square, and together they cover every other unit square.  The two rectangles in column $1$ and column $n$ are empty (the uncovered square is in the topmost or bottommost row), so the total number of non‑empty rectangles is $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$.\\n- The lower bound and the construction match, therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2 = 2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  The grid consists of the set $[n]\\\\times[n]$ of unit squares, where $[n]=\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  Matilda chooses a permutation $\\\\pi\\\\colon [n]\\\\to[n]$ such that the unique uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,\\\\pi(i))$.  For each column $j$ let $r_j$ denote the unique row with $\\\\pi(r_j)=j$; thus the uncovered square in column $j$ is $(r_j,j)$.\\n\\nA rectangular tile is a set $R\\\\times C$ where $R\\\\subseteq[n]$ and $C\\\\subseteq[n]$ are intervals of consecutive integers.  A tile may be placed only if it contains no uncovered square, i.e. for every $i\\\\in R$ we have either $C\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,\\\\pi(i)-1\\\\}$ (the tile lies completely to the **left** of the hole in row $i$) or $C\\\\subseteq\\\\{\\\\pi(i)+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ (the tile lies completely to the **right** of the hole in row $i$).  Consequently each tile is of one of the two following types:\\n\\n* **Left‑type:** there exists a column $c$ such that the tile is $R\\\\times\\\\{1,\\\\dots,c\\\\}$ and $c<\\\\pi(i)$ for every $i\\\\in R$.\\n* **Right‑type:** there exists a column $c$ such that the tile is $R\\\\times\\\\{c,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ and $c>\\\\pi(i)$ for every $i\\\\in R$.\\n\\nWe first prove a lower bound on the number of tiles.\\n\\n#### Lemma 1 (left‑type rectangles).\\nFor each $c\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ there exists at least one left‑type rectangle whose rightmost column is exactly $c$.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Consider the column $c+1$.  By definition of $\\\\pi$ there is a unique row $i_0=r_{c+1}$ with $\\\\pi(i_0)=c+1$.  The square $(i_0,c)$ lies to the left of the uncovered square in its row, hence it must be covered by a left‑type rectangle.  Let $R\\\\times\\\\{1,\\\\dots,d\\\\}$ be the rectangle that covers $(i_0,c)$.  Since the rectangle is left‑type we have $d<\\\\pi(i_0)=c+1$, therefore $d\\\\le c$.  Because the rectangle contains column $c$, we must have $d\\\\ge c$.  Hence $d=c$, i.e. the rectangle’s rightmost column is $c$.  ∎\\n\\n#### Lemma 2 (right‑type rectangles).\\nFor each $c\\\\in\\\\{2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ there exists at least one right‑type rectangle whose leftmost column is exactly $c$.\\n\\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric.  Look at column $c-1$; let $i_0=r_{c-1}$ be the row with $\\\\pi(i_0)=c-1$.  The square $(i_0,c)$ lies to the right of the uncovered square in its row, so it must be covered by a right‑type rectangle $R\\\\times\\\\{e,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  Right‑type forces $e>\\\\pi(i_0)=c-1$, thus $e\\\\ge c$.  Since column $c$ belongs to the rectangle we also have $e\\\\le c$, whence $e=c$.  ∎\\n\\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 we obtain at least $n-1$ distinct left‑type rectangles, one for each $c=1,\\\\dots,n-1$.  Lemma&nbsp;2 yields at least $n-1$ distinct right‑type rectangles, one for each $c=2,\\\\dots,n$.  No rectangle can be simultaneously left‑type and right‑type (otherwise it would contain a hole), so the two families are disjoint.  Consequently any tiling of the board must contain at least\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\n\\\\]\\nrectangular tiles.\\n\\n#### Construction achieving the bound.\\nFor each column $j\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ let $r_j$ be the row of the uncovered square in that column.  Define two rectangles:\\n\\n* $T_j^{\\\\text{up}}$ = the vertical rectangle consisting of all squares $(i,j)$ with $1\\\\le i< r_j$ (the squares **above** the uncovered one);\\n* $T_j^{\\\\text{down}}$ = the vertical rectangle consisting of all squares $(i,j)$ with $r_j< i\\\\le n$ (the squares **below** the uncovered one).\\n\\nBoth rectangles are axis‑aligned, contain no uncovered square, and the two families $\\\\{T_j^{\\\\text{up}}\\\\}$ and $\\\\{T_j^{\\\\text{down}}\\\\}$ are pairwise disjoint.  Their union covers every square that is not uncovered.  For $j=1$ the rectangle $T_1^{\\\\text{up}}$ is empty because the uncovered square in column $1$ lies in the topmost row; similarly $T_n^{\\\\text{down}}$ is empty.  All other $2n-2$ rectangles are non‑empty.  Hence we obtain a tiling with exactly $2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\nSince we have a universal lower bound of $2n-2$ and a construction attaining this bound, the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ this number equals\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the answer is 4048.\\n\\n### End of Detailed Solution ###\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
