es).\\nFor each $c\\\\in\\\\{2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ there exists at least one right‑type rectangle whose leftmost column is exactly $c$.\\n\\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric.  Look at column $c-1$; let $i_0=r_{c-1}$ be the row with $\\\\pi(i_0)=c-1$.  The square $(i_0,c)$ lies to the right of the uncovered square in its row, so it must be covered by a right‑type rectangle $R\\\\times\\\\{e,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  Right‑type forces $e>\\\\pi(i_0)=c-1$, thus $e\\\\ge c$.  Since column $c$ belongs to the rectangle we also have $e\\\\le c$, whence $e=c$.  ∎\\n\\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 we obtain at least $n-1$ distinct left‑type rectangles, one for each $c=1,\\\\dots,n-1$.  Lemma&nbsp;2 yields at least $n-1$ distinct right‑type rectangles, one for each $c=2,\\\\dots,n$.  No rectangle can be simultaneously left‑type and right‑type (otherwise it would contain a hole), so the two families are disjoint.  Consequently any tiling of the board must contain at least\\n\\\\[\\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\n\\\\]\\nrectangular tiles.\\n\\n#### Construction achieving the bound.\\nFor each column $j\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ let $r_j$ be the row of the uncovered square in that column.  Define two rectangles:\\n\\n* $T_j^{\\\\text{up}}$ = the vertical rectangle consisting of all squares $(i,j)$ with $1\\\\le i< r_j$ (the squares **above** the uncovered one);\\n* $T_j^{\\\\text{down}}$ = the vertical rectangle consisting of all squares $(i,j)$ with $r_j< i\\\\le n$ (the squares **below** the uncovered one).\\n\\nBoth rectangles are axis‑aligned, contain no uncovered square, and the two families $\\\\{T_j^{\\\\text{up}}\\\\}$ and $\\\\{T_j^{\\\\text{down}}\\\\}$ are pairwise disjoint.  Their union covers every square that is not uncovered.  For $j=1$ the rectangle $T_1^{\\\\text{up}}$ is empty because the uncovered square in column $1$ lies in the topmost row; similarly $T_n^{\\\\text{down}}$ is empty.  All other $2n-2$ rectangles are non‑empty.  Hence we obtain a tiling with exactly $2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\n