ach row and in each column.  Consequently the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there exists a bijection $\\pi:[n]\\to[n]$ such that the uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,\\pi(i))$.  For each column $j$ let $r_j$ denote the unique row with $\\pi(r_j)=j$; thus the uncovered square in column $j$ is $(r_j,j)$.  The sets $\\{\\pi(i)\\mid i\\in[n]\\}$ and $\\{r_j\\mid j\\in[n]\\}$ are both equal to $[n]$.\n\nA rectangular tile is a product $R\\times C$ where $R\\subseteq[n]$ and $C\\subseteq[n]$ are intervals of consecutive integers.  A tile may be placed only if it contains no uncovered square.  Hence for every row $i\\in R$ we must have either $C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,\\pi(i)-1\\}$ (the tile lies completely to the **left** of the hole in that row) or $C\\subseteq\\{\\pi(i)+1,\\dots,n\\}$ (the tile lies completely to the **right** of the hole in that row).  In particular, if a tile contains a column $c$ and a row $i$ with $\\pi(i)=c$, the tile would contain the uncovered square $(i,c)$ and is therefore forbidden.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.** For each $c\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ the square $A_c=(r_{c+1},c)$ is covered by a tile whose rightmost column is exactly $c$.\n\n*Proof.*  The square $A_c$ lies to the left of the uncovered square $(r_{c+1},c+1)$.  Any tile covering $A_c$ cannot contain column $c+1$, otherwise it would also contain the uncovered square $(r_{c+1},c+1)$.  Consequently the column interval of that tile is a subset of $\\{1,\\dots,c\\}$ and must contain column $c$ (because $A_c$ is covered).  Hence the rightmost column of the tile equals $c$.  \u220e\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** For each $c\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ the square $B_c=(r_c,c+1)$ is covered by a tile whose leftmost column is exactly $c+1$.\n\n*Proof.*  This is the symmetric statement of Lemma\u00a01.  The square $B_c$ lies to the right of the uncovered square $(r_c,c)$.  Any tile covering $B_c$ cannot contain column $c$, otherwise it would contain the uncovered square $(r_c,c)$.  Hence its column interval is a subset of $\\{c+1,\\dots,n\\}$ and must contain column $c+1$, so its leftmost column is $c+1$.  \u220e\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Injectivity.**\n\n*Claim 1.*  If $c\\neq c'$, the tiles given by Lemma\u00a01 for $A_c$ and $A_{c'}$ are distinct.\n\nIndeed, suppose a single tile $T$ covered both $A_c$ and $A_{c'}$ with $c'<c$.  Then $T$ would contain columns $c'$ and $c$, and also the row $r_{c+1}$ (since $A_c$ lies in that row).  Because $T$ contains column $c+1$ as well (its column interval must be contiguous), $T$ would contain the uncovered square $(r_{c+1},c+1)$ \u2013 a contradiction.  The same argument works when $c'>c$.  Hence the mapping $c\\mapsto T(A_c)$ is injective, giving $n-1$ distinct tiles.\n\n*Claim 2.*  The tiles given by Lemma\u00a02 for the squares $B_c$ are also pairwise distinct.  The proof is identical after transposing the board (rows become columns).  Thus we obtain another $n-1$ distinct tiles.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Disjointness of the two families.**\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a tile $T$ serves both as the tile of Lemma\u00a01 for some $A_c$ and as the tile of Lemma\u00a02 for some $B_{c'}$.  The tile $T$ must contain column $c$ (because it covers $A_c$) and column $c'+1$ (because it covers $B_{c'}$).  There are two possibilities.\n\n1. *If $c'=c-1$.*  Then $T$ is forced to have width $1$, i.e. its column interval is exactly $\\{c\\}$.  Since $T$ covers $A_c$, it contains row $r_{c+1}$; since it also covers $B_{c-1}$, it contains row $r_{c-1}$.  The rows of a tile form a consecutive interval, so $T$ must also contain the intermediate row $r_c$.  Consequently $T$ would contain the uncovered square $(r_c,c)$, which is forbidden.\n\n2. *If $c'\\neq c-1$.*  Then $T$ contains at least the two columns $c$ and $c'+1$, hence also column $c+1$.  Because $T$ contains row $r_c$ (from $B_c$) and row $r_{c+1}$ (from $A_c$), its row interval contains the intermediate row $r_c$ or $r_{c+1}$, and therefore $T$ contains one of the uncovered squares $(r_c,c)$ or $(r_{c+1},c+1)$.  Again a contradiction.\n\nThus no tile can belong to both families; the $2(n-1)$ tiles obtained above are all distinct.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lower bound.**\nFrom the two injective families we have proved that any admissible tiling uses at least\n\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\]\ntiles.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Construction attaining the bound.**\nFor each column $j$ let $r_j$ be the row of its uncovered square.  Define two vertical rectangles:\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\nT_j^{\\text{up}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\},\\\\\nT_j^{\\text{down}}&=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBoth rectangles are axis\u2011aligned, contain no uncovered square, and the two families are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different columns.  Their union covers every unit square except the $n$ uncovered ones.\n\nIf $r_j=1$ then $T_j^{\\text{up}}$ is empty; if $r_j=n$ then $T_j^{\\text{down}}$ is empty.  Exactly one column contains the uncovered square in the top row (so $r_j=1$ for exactly one $j$) and exactly one column contains the uncovered square in the bottom row (so $r_j=n$ for exactly one $j$).  Consequently the number of non\u2011empty rectangles is\n\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nThus a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles exists.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**\nThe lower bound $2n-2$ and the explicit construction both give the same value.  For $n=2025$ we obtain\n\\[2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is $\\boxed{4048}$.\n\n### End of Detailed Solution ###"
